{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2131201979/10/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/10`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2131201979/10/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\ndescription：识别网格中每个封闭环的旋转对称阶数，用该阶数在底部颜色图例中查找对应颜色，替换环中心的灰色像素。\n\nInput structure：\n- 背景为 Black。\n- 网格中分布着若干由彩色像素围成的封闭环形对象（环为一像素宽的闭合回路，形状各异），每个环在其几何中心位置有一个待着色的 Grey 像素。\n- 网格底部有一行颜色图例：从左到右依次排列若干种颜色，位置间隔一个背景像素。第 1 个颜色对应旋转对称阶数 1（即无旋转对称），第 2 个对应阶数 2（即 180° 旋转后与自身重合），第 4 个对应阶数 4（即 90° 旋转后与自身重合），中间未使用的位置为背景色。\n\nRule：\n- 对每个封闭环，检测其像素集合的旋转对称性：将环的像素集合分别绕其几何中心旋转 90°、180°、270°，找到最高的旋转对称阶数 k（即旋转 360°/k 后形状与自身完全重合的最大 k 值，k 取自 1、2、4）。\n- 在底部图例中找到第 k 个位置的颜色。\n- 将环中心的 Grey 像素替换为该颜色。\n- 环本身、图例和背景保持不变。\n\nKey insight：解题者必须先识别每个环的旋转对称阶数这一几何属性，再通过图例将阶数映射为颜色——对称性判断和图例查找构成两层独立的推理。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/e729b7be, eval/f560132c, train/46c35fc7, train/f0f8a26d, train/6350f1f4, train/6165ea8f, train/3bd292e8, train/ad3b40cf\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e729b7be\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3242\nmechanism=0.4125\nlexical=0.0184\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e729b7be.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e729b7be.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.\n- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.\n- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.\nRule\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.\nKey insight\n- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"colored foreground object pixels excluding the center-column divider and center-row markers\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy each source pixel to its 180-degree point-reflection through the center marker, preserving the original pixels, divider, and markers\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2843\nmechanism=0.3131\nlexical=0.0140\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, object:template, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, relation:between, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/f560132c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f560132c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Assemble scattered sprites into a composite shape using a legend sprite that specifies colors for each component position.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"monochrome sprites scattered in the grid s\",\n  \"dst\": \"assemble into composite shape matching the legend sprite's silhouette d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.shape matches d.region.shape (d is always in top-left, final assembly forms rectangle)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.color = legend[s.position_in_composite].color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"legend sprite contains 2x2 colored pixels where each pixel's position (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right) maps to corresponding sprite's recolor in final assembly\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"tight crop\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: identify legend sprite (contains multi-color 2x2 pattern); Step 2: identify other monochrome sprites with same shape structure; Step 3: assemble sprites into composite matching legend silhouette; Step 4: recolor each assembled region based on legend position mapping\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"jigsaw\",\n    \"color-legend\",\n    \"assembly\",\n    \"spatial-correspondence\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"46c35fc7\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2923\nmechanism=0.3366\nlexical=0.0168\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:rotate, output:same_size, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/46c35fc7.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/46c35fc7.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate the colors around every 3x3 foreground ring, with corners and side-centers cycling in opposite directions.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Each 4-connected foreground object is a complete 3x3 ring: eight foreground pixels around a background center.\n\nRule\n- For each ring, move corner colors one step around the four corners.\n- Move side-center colors one step around the four side centers in the opposite direction.\n- Leave the background center and all other background pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Corners and side centers form two separate four-cycles on the same 3x3 ring.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected 3x3 foreground rings with a background center\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each ring, cycle the corner colors one step around the corners and cycle the side-center colors one step around the side centers in the opposite direction\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f0f8a26d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3097\nmechanism=0.3616\nlexical=0.0190\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, output:same_size, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f0f8a26d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f0f8a26d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.\nEach 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical\nbar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.\n\nRule\nClear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,\nand color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.\n\nKey insight\nThe bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output\nplacement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected monochromatic foreground bars on a dominant background\",\n  \"transform\": \"rotate each odd-length horizontal or vertical bar 90 degrees about its midpoint; preserve its color and length, with single-pixel bars unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6350f1f4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2797\nmechanism=0.2875\nlexical=0.0213\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:reflect, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/6350f1f4.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/6350f1f4.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDecode a block-grid image by swapping each sprite-coded cell with a solid cell and each blank cell with the sprite.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is partitioned by black separator rows and columns into equal-size\nsquare blocks. Each block is either a copy of one two-color sprite or a\nuniform blank block of the sprite's minority color; grey pixels are erased\ncells that act as wildcards.\n\nRule\nInfer the repeated sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks. Replace a\nblock matching that sprite, ignoring grey erasures, with a solid block of\nthe sprite's majority color. Replace a uniform blank block with the sprite.\nSeparator rows and columns, including grey erasures on them, become black.\n\nKey insight\nThe small sprite is a block-level code for one macro-cell state, and the\nuniform minority-color block is the other state; the output renders the\nopposite code in each macro-cell while cleaning erased pixels.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"regular block grid separated by black lines; each block is either a two-color sprite or a uniform blank block, with grey pixels as erasures\",\n  \"dst\": \"same block grid with the two block states swapped\",\n  \"transform\": \"infer the sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks; sprite-coded blocks become solid majority-color blocks, blank blocks become the sprite, and separator/erasure pixels become black\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6165ea8f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2935\nmechanism=0.3509\nlexical=0.0172\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, object:template, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/6165ea8f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/6165ea8f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nBuild a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.\n- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.\n- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.\n\nRule:\n- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.\n- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.\n- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.\n- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.\n\nKey insight:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"题目结构已经拆开了：求解器会把底部图例解析成 `1/2/4 -> 颜色`，再按每个环的灰色中心做旋转对称检测。接下来我先用几个原型形状验证“矩形减角再取边界”这条生成路线，确认它能稳定产出 1、2、4 阶三类闭环。"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport numpy as np\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef boundary(solid):\n    h,w=solid.shape\n    out=np.zeros_like(solid)\n    for r in range(h):\n        for c in range(w):\n            if not solid[r,c]:\n                continue\n            for dr,dc in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:\n                rr,cc=r+dr,c+dc\n                if rr<0 or rr>=h or cc<0 or cc>=w or not solid[rr,cc]:\n                    out[r,c]=True\n                    break\n    return out\n\ndef comps(mask):\n    h,w=mask.shape\n    seen=np.zeros_like(mask,bool)\n    n=0\n    for r in range(h):\n        for c in range(w):\n            if not mask[r,c] or seen[r,c]:\n                continue\n            n+=1\n            dq=deque([(r,c)])\n            seen[r,c]=1\n            while dq:\n                x,y=dq.popleft()\n                for dx in (-1,0,1):\n                    for dy in (-1,0,1):\n                        if dx==dy==0: continue\n                        xx,yy=x+dx,y+dy\n                        if 0<=xx<h and 0<=yy<w and mask[xx,yy] and not seen[xx,yy]:\n                            seen[xx,yy]=1; dq.append((xx,yy))\n    return n\n\ndef enclosed(mask):\n    h,w=mask.shape\n    pr=np.pad(mask,1)\n    seen=np.zeros_like(pr,bool)\n    dq=deque([(0,0)])\n    seen[0,0]=1\n    while dq:\n        r,c=dq.popleft()\n        for dr,dc in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:\n            rr,cc=r+dr,c+dc\n            if 0<=rr<h+2 and 0<=cc<w+2 and not pr[rr,cc] and not seen[rr,cc]:\n                seen[rr,cc]=1; dq.append((rr,cc))\n    cr,cc=h//2+1,w//2+1\n    return (not pr[cr,cc]) and (not seen[cr,cc])\n\ndef order(mask):\n    h,w=mask.shape\n    cr,cc=h//2,w//2\n    pts={(r-cr,c-cc) for r,c in zip(*np.where(mask))}\n    def rot90(p):\n        r,c=p; return (-c,r)\n    rot90_pts={rot90(p) for p in pts}\n    rot180_pts={(-r,-c) for r,c in pts}\n    return 4 if rot90_pts==pts else 2 if rot180_pts==pts else 1\n\ndef solid_notches(h,w,tl,tr,bl,br):\n    s=np.ones((h,w),dtype=bool)\n    if tl: s[:tl,:tl]=0\n    if tr: s[:tr,w-tr:]=0\n    if bl: s[h-bl:,:bl]=0\n    if br: s[h-br:,w-br:]=0\n    return s\n\ncases=[('4',7,7,1,1,1,1),('4b',9,9,2,2,2,2),('2',5,7,0,0,0,0),('2b',7,7,2,1,1,2),('1',7,7,2,0,1,0)]\nfor name,h,w,tl,tr,bl,br in cases:\n    s=solid_notches(h,w,tl,tr,bl,br)\n    b=boundary(s)\n    print(name,'order',order(b),'comp',comps(b),'enc',enclosed(b),'center',b[h//2,w//2])\n    print(b.astype(int))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
